  "context_pill": "Opting Out u/s 115BAC(6) & Section 87A Rebate",
  "example_summary": "Computation of tax liability for Mr. Manish (age 47, resident) with income of ₹ 4,15,000 opting out of default regime.",
  "source_provision": "### Statutory Framework: Old Tax Regime & Section 87A Rebate\n\n| Provision / Section | Operative Condition / Criteria | Tax Rate / Rebate Quantum |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Section 115BAC(6)** | Opting out of Default Tax Regime | Normal Slab Rates applicable |\n| **Basic Exemption Limit** | Resident Individual aged < 60 years | Income up to ₹ 2,50,000 is Exempt |\n| **First Slab (₹ 2,50,001 - ₹ 5,00,000)** | Income exceeding Basic Exemption | Taxed @ 5% |\n| **Section 87A Rebate** | Resident Individual with Total Income ≤ ₹ 5,00,000 | Lower of Tax Payable or ₹ 12,500 |\n\n#### Key Statutory Checklists\n\n- **Regime Shift Option u/s 115BAC(6)**\n  - Assessee must explicitly exercise option to shift out of default regime on or before due date u/s 139(1).\n  - Switching back rules depend on whether the individual has Income from Business or Profession.\n\n- **Section 87A Rebate Conditions (Old Tax Regime)**\n  - Assessee MUST be a **Resident Individual** (Non-residents and non-individuals cannot claim).\n  - Total Income after all deductions under Chapter VI-A must **not exceed ₹ 5,00,000**.\n  - Rebate equals **100% of tax liability** or **₹ 12,500**, whichever is lower, reducing final tax to Nil.\n\n> [!IMPORTANT] Section 87A Distinction\n> Under the Old Tax Regime, Rebate u/s 87A is capped at ₹ 12,500 for Total Income up to ₹ 5,00,000. Under the Default Tax Regime u/s 115BAC, the threshold is higher (Total Income up to ₹ 7,00,000 with maximum rebate of ₹ 25,000).",
